          %h2#sphere_packing_problem Sphere packing problem
          %p
            The
            %em sphere packing problem
            is the problem of finding the densest possible sphere packings in any dimension.
          %h3#low_dimensional_cases Low dimensional cases
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1 = 1
            the problem is trivial.
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1 = 2
            it is relatively easy to show that the densest packing is given by spheres centered on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/honeycomb+lattice"} honeycomb lattice
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1 = 3
            it used to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kepler%27s+conjecture"} Kepler's conjecture
            that the densest packing is that given by spheres centered on the cubical lattice. This was proven only with ample computer assistance by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Flyspeck+project"} Flyspeck project
          %h3#exceptional_cases Exceptional cases
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1 = 8
            the denest packing is that of spheres centered on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E8-lattice"} E8-lattice
            (
            %a{:href => "#Viazovska16"}> Viazovska 16
            ).
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %mn 24
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1 = 24
            the densists packing is that of spheres centered on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leech+lattice"} Leech lattice
            (
            %a{:href => "#CKMRV17"}> CKMRV 17
            ).
          %p
            An interpretation of the ingredients entering these proofs in terms of bounds on scaling dimensions of fields in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theories"} conformal field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT"} AdS/CFT
            as related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+gravity+conjecture"} weak gravity conjecture
            in AdS-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"}> quantum gravity
            , is due to (
            %a{:href => "#HMR19"}> HMR 19
            ).
          %h3#irregular_cases Irregular cases
          %p
            For other values of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            exact solutions remain unknown, but numerlical simulation shows that the densest sphere packings in the remaining dimensions will not be controlled by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+in+a+vector+space"} lattices
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#Engel86
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Engel"}> Peter Engel
                , Chapter 10 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric Crystallography – An Axiomatic Introduction to Crystallography
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-009-4760-3"} doi:10.1007/978-94-009-4760-3
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (which is otherwise concerned with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallography"} crystallography
                  via
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+groups"} crystallographic groups
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sphere_packing"} Sphere packing
          %p Solution to the sphere packing problem in dimensions 8 and 24:
          %ul
            %li#Viazovska16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maryna+Viazovska"}> Maryna Viazovska
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The sphere packing problem in dimension 8
                %strong 185
                3 (2017) 991-1015 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.04246"}> arXiv:1603.04246
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2017.185.3.7"} doi:10.4007/annals.2017.185.3.7
            %li#CKMRV17
              %p
                Henry Cohn, Abhinav Kumar, Stephen D. Miller, Danylo Radchenko,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maryna+Viazovska"} Maryna Viazovska
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The sphere packing problem in dimension 24
                %strong 185
                (2017) 1017-1033 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.06518"}> arXiv:1603.06518
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2017.185.3.8"} doi:10.4007/annals.2017.185.3.8
          %p Exposition:
          %ul
            %li
              Ursula Whitcher,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Eight-dimensional spheres and the exceptional
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mn 8
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_8
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://mathvoices.ams.org/featurecolumn/2022/09/01/eight-dimensional-spheres-and-the-exceptional-e_8/"} web
          %h3#ReferencesInStringTheory In string theory
          %p
            An interpretation of the ingredients entering these proofs in terms of bounds on scaling dimensions of fields in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theories"} conformal field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT"} AdS/CFT
            as related to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+gravity+conjecture"} weak gravity conjecture
            in AdS-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"}> quantum gravity
            , is due to
          %ul
            %li#HMR19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Hartman"}> Thomas Hartman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dalimil+Maz%C3%A1%C4%8D"} Dalimil Mazáč
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leonardo+Rastelli"} Leonardo Rastelli
              %em Sphere Packing and Quantum Gravity
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.01319"}> arXiv:1905.01319
